derivations.claim:
Choose a linear extension $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m$ of the finite poset in which every maximal remaining element is listed next. The prefix $U_k=\{\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_k\}$ is upward closed, so $J_k=\operatorname{span}\{C^\mu_{s,t}:\mu\in U_k\}$ is an ideal: it is the sum of the ideals $A^{\ge\mu}$ for $\mu\in U_k$. Also $J_k=J_{k-1}+A^{\ge\lambda_k}$, because every label strictly above $\lambda_k$ occurs earlier. Let $S$ be a simple right $A$-module. Since $J_m=A$, choose the least $k$ with $SJ_k
e0$. Then $SJ_{k-1}=0$ and $SJ_k=S$, so $SA^{\ge\lambda_k}=S$ and $SA^{>\lambda_k}=0$. Choose $s_0\in S$ and $u$ such that $s_0C^{\lambda_k}_{u,v}
e0$ for some $v$; this is possible because $SA^{\ge\lambda_k}
e0$ and the cell basis spans that ideal. Define $\psi:\Delta(\lambda_k)	o S$ by $\psi(c_t)=s_0C^{\lambda_k}_{u,t}$. For $a\in A$, the cell axiom gives $C^{\lambda_k}_{u,t}a\equiv\sum_w r_{t,w}(a)C^{\lambda_k}_{u,w}$ modulo $A^{>\lambda_k}$; that higher ideal annihilates $S$, so $\psi(c_ta)=\psi(c_t)a$. The map is a nonzero module map and hence surjective. Choose $t$ with $\psi(c_t)
e0$. The annihilator $\{x\in S:xA^{\ge\lambda_k}=0\}$ is a submodule because $A^{\ge\lambda_k}$ is a two-sided ideal. It is proper since $SA^{\ge\lambda_k}=S
e0$, so simplicity makes it zero. Hence $\psi(c_t)A^{\ge\lambda_k}
e0$; since $SA^{>\lambda_k}=0$, some cell basis element $C^{\lambda_k}_{p,q}$ acts nontrivially on $\psi(c_t)$. If the form on $\Delta(\lambda_k)$ were zero, the rank-one action identity would give $c_tC^{\lambda_k}_{p,q}=0$, contradicting the module-map identity $\psi(c_tC^{\lambda_k}_{p,q})=\psi(c_t)C^{\lambda_k}_{p,q}
e0$. Thus the form is nonzero and its radical is proper. By step 1.1, every proper submodule of $\Delta(\lambda_k)$ is contained in its radical; since $\ker\psi$ is maximal, it equals the radical. Therefore $S\cong D(\lambda_k)$.
